## Introduction
Photosynthesis, the conversion of light energy into physiologically available chemical energy, is accomplished by a group of membrane-integral protein complexes: the photosynthetic reaction center complexes or photosynthetic systems. Each contains a central catalytic domain, henceforth referred to as the reaction centre (RC) or RC-core. Apart from eukaryotic plastids, phototrophic species are widely distributed amongst the phyla of the (eu)bacterial domain (Woese, 1987; Woese et al., 1990) including the cyanobacteria (and prochlorophytes), the green sulfur bacteria, the green ®lamentous bacteria, the purple bacteria and the heliobacteria (see Table 1). Of these, only the cyanobacteria and prochlorophytes are capable of oxygenic photosynthesis i.e. they liberate molecular oxygen through water oxidation (Figure 1). All remaining photosynthetic bacteria are anoxygenic.
According to the endosymbiotic theory, cyanobacteria-like cells were incorporated into the eukaryotic systems and developed into the plastids of algae and higher plants (Margulis, 1981, 1996; Stiller & Hall, 1997) thereby extending oxygenic photosynthesis into the eukarya.
All bacterial photosynthetic systems identi®ed to date belong to two distinct groups, conveniently classi®ed by the respective terminal electron acceptors of the electron transfer systems. Type-I (alternatively iron-sulfur type) RC-complexes possess high-potential $\mathrm{Fe}_{4}S_{4}$ -clusters as their terminal electron acceptors and include the photosystems (PS) of anoxygenic heliobacteria and green sulfur bacteria as well as photosystem I (PSI) of oxygenic photosynthesis (Prince et al., 1985; Nitschke et al., 1987; Trost & Blankenship, 1989; Trost et al., 1992). Photosynthetic systems of type-II (quinone-type) are characterised by a quinone-type terminal electron acceptor. This group is constituted by the RCcomplexes of green ®lamentous bacteria (GbRC), purple bacteria (PbRC) and photosystem II (PSII) of oxygenic photosynthesis (Barber, 1988; Rutherford, 1988; Mathis, 1990; Nitschke & Rutherford, 1991; Blankenship, 1992). Whereas anoxygenic bacteria posses a single RC-complex, either of type-I or of type-II, oxygenic cyanobacteria electrochemically link two photosynthetic systems (one of each type) in series. As a result they are able to oxidise water and reduce $\mathrm{{NADP^{+}}}$ at the two extremes of the electrochemical range accessible in vivo.
Yet another characteristic feature serves to classify the photosynthetic systems, i.e. the possession of a core antenna system (or alternatively the lack thereof). Both PSI and PSII of oxygenic photosynthesis share this feature with the type-I RC-complexes from heliobacteria and green sulfur bacteria. The latter will correspondingly be referred to as photosystems throughout this text, abbreviated as PSH and PSC. The H and C derive from the corresponding genus, gene and protein names (H - Heliobacillus, pshA and PshA etc.; C - Chlorobium, pscA and PscA etc.; Bryant, 1994; Sakurai et al., 1996). The only photosynthetic systems without core antennae are the mutually related type-II complexes of purple bacteria (PbRC) and green ®lamentous bacteria (GbRC). Table 1 summarises the subdivisions discussed above and provides the names of core proteins and antenna-binding proteins, as currently in general use.
The ®rst three-dimensional structure of an RCcomplex (a type-II RC) that was determined was that of the purple bacterium Rhodopseudomonas viridis (Deisenhofer et al., 1985, 1995), followed by the related structure of Rhodobacter sphaeroides (Allen et al., 1986; Chang et al., 1991; Ermler et al., 1994; Arnoux et al., 1989, 1995). The most detailed structural information for type-I systems is available from a 4 ÊA structural model of photosystem I (Krauû et al., 1996; Schubert et al., 1997: PDB-code 2pps).
Now that a representative model of each of the two RC types exists, a comparison of the two is opportune. As will be demonstrated, both share far-reaching structural analogies. The striking structural analogy between the RC-cores of both structures con®rms the long-standing hypothesis of a common evolutionary origin for all RC-complexes (e.g. see Nitschke & Rutherford, 1991; Blankenship, 1992). Nevertheless, despite the observed similarity in structure, the differences between the two are no less signi®cant and constitute a major part of the discussion.
Coupled to the recent report of amino acid sequence homology of the core-antenna-binding proteins, CP43 and CP47 of PSII, PshA of PSH, and PsaA and PsaB of PSI (Vermaas, 1994; Fromme et al., 1996) structural models of the RCcomplexes not yet investigated at moderate to high resolution will be developed or expanded upon.
## Results and Discussion
## The structure of photosystem I
The cyanobacterial PSI-monomer consists of 11 protein subunits, of which eight (PsaA, PsaB, PsaF and PsaI to PsaM) are membrane-integral and three (PsaC to PsaE) are stromally extrinsic (Figure 2; Krauû et al, 1996; Schubert et al., 1997). Of these, only PsaA, PsaB and PsaC bind the cofactors of the electron transfer system. PsaC binds the terminal $\mathrm{Fe}_{4}S_{4}$ -clusters, $\mathrm{F_{A}}$ and $\mathrm{F_{B},}$ of PSI, which are without counterpart in the type-II RCs (see above). PsaA and PsaB, two highly homologous proteins (Fish et al., 1985), each consists of two sequential and structural domains: An N-terminal, peripheral, antenna-binding domain and a C-terminal, central RC-domain. The latter domains coordinate the cofactors of the electron transfer system and are consequently of relevance in functionally comparing the RC-core to that of the purple bacteria (Figure 2). The N-terminal, antenna-binding domains of PsaA and PsaB will be considered when comparing the core-antennae of the various photosystems.
Spectroscopically the following cofactors have been identi®ed as part of the electron transfer system of PSI (for reviews, see Brettel, 1997; Malkin, 1996; SeÂtif, 1992): A primary electron donor P700 (a Chla-species), a primary electron acceptor $\mathbf{A}_{0}$ (a monomeric Chla), a secondary electron acceptor $\mathbf{A}_{1}$ (a phylloquinone) and three terminal acceptors $\mathrm{F}_{\mathrm{X}},$ $\mathrm{F_{A}}$ and $F_{\mathrm{B}}^{\mathrm{^{-}}}$ (three $\mathrm{Fe}_{4}S_{4}$ -clusters). Structurally, two sets of pseudosymmetrically arranged cofactors are observed (Figure 3, left; Schubert et al., 1997), consisting of a lumenal dimer of Chla $(\mathrm{eC_{1}}$ and $\mathrm{eC_{1}}^{\prime}$ , P700), two pseudosymmetric pairs of Chla-monomers $\mathrm{eC}_{2}$ and ${\mathrm{eC}}_{2}^{\prime}$ as well as $\bar{\mathrm{eC}}_{3}$ and ${\mathrm{eC}}_{3}^{\prime}$ (at least one probably equivalent to $\begin{array}{r}{\mathbf{A}_{0}\mathbf{,}}\end{array}$ ), a single phylloquinone position $\hat{\mathrm{Q_{K}}}$ (possibly equivalent to $\dot{\mathrm{A_{1}}})$ , and three iron-sulfur clusters $\mathrm{{\bar{F}}_{X},\ \mathrm{{\bar{F}}_{1}}}$ and $\mathrm{F}_{2}$ (the latter two equivalent to $\mathrm{F_{A}}$ and $\mathrm{F_{B}},$ ). The pseudo-twofold axis is referred to as ${\mathrm{C}}_{2}(\mathrm{AB})$ . At present it is uncertain whether the breakdown of pseudosymmetry at the position of the single quinone, $\scriptstyle\mathrm{Q}_{\mathrm{K}},$ is of any
