## The structure of the purple bacterial reaction center
Of all photosynthetic systems, those of the purple bacteria have by far been best characterised spectroscopically, biochemically and structurally (for reviews, see Deisenhofer & Michel, 1993; Lancaster et al., 1995). Overall, the PbRC consists of either three or four subunits: two partly homologous, membrane-integral subunits L and M, a cytoplasmic subunit H and in some species (including Rhodopseudomonas viridis) a periplasmic, tetraheme cytochrome subunit C (Figure 2). The subunit H has no counterpart in any of the other ${\mathsf{R C s}},$ while the situation with respect to a periplasmic cytochrome subunit as regards the green sulfur bacteria is still unclear (Hager-Braun et al., 1995, 1997; Albouy et al., 1997). These subunits are not present in PSI and will not be further discussed. The subunits L and M form a heterodimer, with extensive pseudosymmetry between the two halves. This axis will be referred to as ${\mathrm{C}}_{2}(\mathrm{LM})$ .
In total, the nine cofactors common to all PbRC are arranged in two pseudosymmetric branches around the ${\mathrm{C}}_{2}(\mathrm{LM})$ -axis. Starting from the periplasmic side these include: (i) a dimer of BChl, $\mathrm{\DeltaD_{L}}^{-}$ and $\mathrm{D}_{\mathrm{M}},$ the primary electron donor or \`\`special pair''; (ii) a pseudosymmetric pair of accessory BChl, $\mathtt{B_{A}}$ and $\hat{\mathrm{B_{\mathrm{B}}}};$ (iii) a pseudosymmetric pair of bacteriopheophytin, $\mathrm{\overline{{H_{A}}}}^{\overline{{}}}$ and $\mathrm{H}_{\mathrm{B}}\dot{\mathbf{\Omega}},$ (iv) a pair of quinones $\mathrm{Q_{A}}$ and $\dot{\mathrm{Q}}_{\mathrm{B}}\dot{,}$ and (v) a non-heme iron Fe, located on the ${\mathrm{C}}_{2}(\mathrm{LM})$ -axis (Figure $^{3,}$ right). While the exact function of the $\mathtt{B_{A}}$ is still under discussion (Zinth et al., 1996), both $\mathsf{B}_{\mathrm{B}}$ and $\mathrm{\DeltaH_{B}}$ are not reduced under physiological conditions i.e. only branch A is active in electron transfer. This does not hold for the quinones $\mathrm{Q_{A}}$ and $\mathrm{Q}_{\mathrm{B}},$ both of which sequentially participate in electron transfer. $\mathrm{\DeltaQ_{A}}$ is a tightly bound, single-electron acceptor, consecutively passing two electrons to $\scriptstyle\mathrm{Q}_{\mathrm{B}}$ . The latter binds two protons in the process and diffuses out of the RC into the membrane phase as a quinol to be replaced by an unreduced quinone molecule.
The differences between the individual purple bacterial RC structures have been analysed (Ermler et al., 1994) and will not be repeated here. For the purpose of comparing the PbRC to PSI, we are especially interested in the guiding principles rather than particular, intricate details. We have chosen the structure of Rhodopseudomonas viridis (Deisenhofer et al., 1985, 1995) for this purpose, especially as this represents the structure known at the highest resolution, i.e. $2.3\mathrm{~\AA~}$ .
Note that in oxygenic (cyanobacterial and eukaryotic) plastids the photosynthetic apparatus is located in a specialised membrane system, the thylakoid membrane, which encloses and de®nes a dedicated region, the lumen. In anoxygenic bacteria, the protein complexes required for photosynthetic activity are embedded within the inner of two cell membranes. Thus, whereas the positive side (p-side) of the anoxygenic RC-complexes faces the intermembranal space or periplasm, it is associated with the thylakoid lumen in oxygenic photosynthetic systems. By contrast, the negative side (nside) of all bacterial (i.e. all anoxygenic and cyanobacterial) RC-complexes is directed towards the cytoplasm. In the eukaryotic plastids, on the other hand, the thylakoids are located within the stromal phase, with the result that the n-side of eukaryotic RC-complexes faces the stroma (Figure 2). To avoid repeated listings of equivalent terms, the p-side, lumen and periplasm will be used synonymously throughout the text, as will n-side, cytoplasm and stroma.
## The electron transfer systems of PSI and the PbRC
The analogy between the two RC-cores of alternative types is most immediately accessible when comparing the electron transfer systems (Figure 3): Both contain a pseudosymmetric arrangement of three pairs of porphyrin-type molecules followed by a pair of quinones (one of which has not been located in PSI) and an irontype cofactor, located on the pseudo-2-fold axis. Despite this similarity, the differences in chemical identity, cofactor orientations and the edge-to-edge distances between the cofactors are in some instances appreciable.
## The primary electron donors
The primary electron donors and their immediate surroundings are the most highly conserved features between the two structural RC-prototypes PSI and PbRC (see Figures 3 and 4a). In moving away from this point both toward the n-side and radially outward from the central pseudo- ${\bf{\mathcal{C}}}_{2}$ axes, deviations from strict analogy become perceptibly larger.
Following the publication of the PbRC X-ray structure, a dimeric arrangement of (bacterio)chlorophylls was proposed for the primary electron donor of most photosynthetic systems (Nitschke & Rutherford, 1991). For P700, the primary electron donor of PSI, this was established by the $6.0\textup{\AA}$ structural model (Krauû et al., 1993). At $4\mathring{\mathrm{A}}$ resolution, the perpendicular interplanar distance between $\mathrm{eC_{1}}$ and $\mathrm{eC_{1}}^{\prime}$ of $3.8(\pm0.5)\$ ÊA and the centre-to-centre distance of $7.5(\pm1.0)\$ ÊA are observed to be quite similar to the $3.{\dot{7}}\mathrm{\AA}$ and $7.6\mathrm{~\AA~}$ reported for $\bar{R}$ . viridis (Ermler et al., 1994; Deisenhofer et al., 1995). The inclination of the (B)Chl planes of the primary electron donors relative to the membrane normal are $1.8^{\circ}$ and $3.2^{\circ}$ for $\mathrm{\DeltaD_{L}}$ and $\mathrm{\DeltaD_{M}}$ in PbRC and $4(\pm5)^{\circ}$ for both $\mathrm{eC_{1}}$ and $\mathrm{eC_{1}}^{\prime}$ in PSI. Thus within the margins of error (signi®cantly larger for PSI) the positioning of the porphyrin planes of both primary electron donors relative to each other are strikingly similar.
Clearly, the primary electron donors of both PSI and PbRC are structurally well conserved despite belonging to different RC-types. This indicates that the spatial parameters for a (B)Chl-dimer are especially stringent for it to successfully function as an electron donor in charge separation. Consequently, we would expect the primary electron donors of the GbRC, PSH and PSC to be very similar to those of PSI and PbRC. Whether and to what degree PSII forms an exception to this rule due to the highly positive redox potential of $\mathrm{P680^{+}}\cdot$ (as discussed by Mulkidjanian et al., 1996; Diner & Babcock, 1996) remains to be demonstrated.
## The accessory (B)chlorophyll pairs
Structurally, $\mathrm{eC}_{2}$ and ${\mathrm{eC}}_{2}^{\prime}$ of PSI are to some degree analogously positioned to the accessory $\mathtt{B_{A}}$ and $\mathsf{B}_{\mathrm{B}}$ of PbRC. Accordingly, the term accessory was adopted for $\mathrm{eC}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{e}\breve{C}_{2}^{\dot{\prime}}$ as well (Krauû et al., 1993). The functional role of these monomeric BChl in purple bacteria has been the source of much debate (Marcus, 1987; Arlt et al., 1993; Small, 1995; Zinth et al., 1996). The broadly analogous positioning of $\mathrm{eC}_{2}/\mathrm{eC}_{2}^{\prime}$ and $\mathrm{\DeltaB_{A}/B_{B}}$ would imply their functions to be largely similar. However, because $\mathrm{eC}_{2}$ (underscoring implies both $\mathrm{eC}_{2}$ and ${\mathrm{eC}}_{2}^{\prime}$ , etc.) is roughly parallel with and in close proximity to the vector $\underline{{\mathrm{\bar{e}C_{1}}}}\underline{{\mathrm{-eC}}}_{3},$ its participation in electron transfer during charge separation seems especially likely (Krauû et al., 1996; Schubert et al., 1997), possibly even more so than in the case of PbRC, as $\mathtt{B_{A}}$ in the latter is not positioned as close to the vector connecting $\mathrm{D}_{\mathrm{L}}/\mathrm{D}_{\mathrm{M}}^{\bullet}$ and $\mathrm{\DeltaH_{A}}$ .
If projected onto the membrane plane, the center-of-mass positions of the accessory (B)Chl are comparable in both PSI and PbRC (Figure 3 bottom and Figure 4b). The inclination and membrane depth, by contrast, deviate markedly from strict analogy: (i) $\mathtt{B_{A}}$ and $\mathsf{B}_{\mathrm{B}}$ of $\mathrm{PbRC}$ are inclined by only $\bar{37.0^{\circ}}$ and $33.9^{\circ}$ relative to the membrane plane. At $62(\pm5)^{\circ}$ the inclinations of $\mathrm{eC}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{eC}_{2}^{\prime}$ relative to the membrane plane are intermediate between those of $\mathrm{eC_{1}}$ and $\underline{{\mathrm{e}\hat{\mathrm{C}}_{3}}}$ and are signi®cantly more closely aligned with the membrane normal than $\mathrm{B_{A}/B_{B}}$ . (ii) Relative to the mean membrane depth of the primary electron donor P860, the accessory $\mathrm{BCh}\mathbf{\hat{B}}_{\mathrm{A}}$ and $\mathsf{B}_{\mathrm{B}}$ of PbRC are located $3\mathrm{~\AA~}$ nearer the $\mathrm{\tt{p}}$ -side surface than the comparable $\mathrm{eC}_{2}$ and ${\mathrm{eC}}_{2}^{\prime}$ in PSI (see Figure 3).
## The third tetrapyrrole pair
At least one of the Chla, $\mathrm{eC}_{3}$ and ${\mathrm{eC}}_{3}^{\prime}$ , is assumed to be equivalent to $\mathbf{A}_{0},$ the spectroscopically identi®ed primary electron acceptor of PSI (Schubert et al., 1995). Similarly to $\operatorname{eC}_{1},\ \operatorname{eC}_{1}{}^{\prime}$ and $\mathrm{eC}_{2},$ $\mathrm{eC}_{2}^{\prime}.$ , which have approximate analogues in PbRC, this third pair of Chl in PSI is, to some degree, analogously positioned to bacteriopheophytins $\mathrm{\DeltaH_{A}}$ and $\mathrm{\ddot{H_{B}}}$ in purple bacteria. (i) Depthwise, $\mathrm{\DeltaH_{A}}$ and $\mathrm{\DeltaH_{B}}$ are located ${\sim}2\mathrm{~\AA~}$ nearer the primary electron donor than $\mathrm{eC}_{3}$ and ${\mathrm{eC}}_{3}^{\prime}$ (ii) Compared to PbRC, the perpendicular height difference (along the membrane normal) between the second and third porphyrin-type cofactor is ${\sim}1$ to $2\textup{\AA}$ shorter in PSI. (iii) The porphyrin planes of $\mathrm{\DeltaH_{A}}$ and $\mathrm{\DeltaH_{B}}$ are noticeably less inclined $24^{\circ}$ for both relative to the membrane normal) than is the case for $\mathrm{eC}_{3}$ and $\mathrm{eC}_{3}^{\prime}$ whose plane inclinations are $43^{\circ}$ and $44^{\circ}(\pm5^{\circ})$ respectively. (iv) The positions within the membrane plane are signi®cantly different (see Figure $\mathsf{4c}),$ ): In a view from the $\mathrm{\tt{p}}$ -side, the line $\mathrm{H}_{\mathrm{A}}–\mathrm{H}_{\mathrm{B}} $ is rotated counterclockwise around the pseudo-2-fold symmetry axis ${\bf C}_{2}({\bf L M})$ in comparison to ${\mathrm{eC}}_{3}{\mathrm{-eC}}_{3}^{\prime}$ . $(\mathsf{C}_{2}(\mathrm{LM})$ and ${\mathrm{C}}_{2}(\mathrm{AB})$ roughly correspond to the non-heme Fe (black dot in Figure 4a and b). As a result, $\mathrm{\DeltaH_{A}}$ and $\mathrm{\DeltaH_{B}}$ are each located appreciably nearer one of $\mathrm{\DeltaD_{L}}$ and $\mathrm{\DeltaD_{M}}$ (center-to-center) than to the other (Figure 3b). In PSI, by contrast, $\mathrm{eC}_{3}$ and ${\mathrm{eC}}_{3}^{\prime}$ are each approximately equidistant from either $\mathrm{eC_{1}}$ or $\mathrm{eC_{1}}^{\prime}$ (Figure 3a).
## Quinone electron acceptors
In comparing $\mathrm{Q}_{\mathrm{K}}$ of PSI to $\mathrm{Q_{A}}$ in PbRC, a signi®- cant difference in quinone acceptor positions is apparent. The difference in membrane depth between the quinone acceptors $\mathrm{Q}_{\mathrm{K}}$ and $\mathrm{\DeltaQ_{A}}$ relative to their respective primary electron donors is approximately $3.5(\pm0.5)\ \mathring{\mathrm{A}},$ shorter in PSI than in $\mathrm{P}\mathrm{\hat{b}}\mathrm{\hat{R}}\mathrm{C}$ . In addition, $Q_{\mathrm{A}}$ of PbRC is nearer the pseu${\mathrm{do}}{\mathrm{-}}C_{2}$ axis $({\sim}8.7\mathrm{\AA})$ than $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Q}}_{\mathrm{K}}$ of PSI $(\sim10.6\mathrm{\AA})$ , clearly re¯ecting the requirement of quinone-quinone $\mathrm{\tilde{(Q_{A}-Q_{B})}}$ electron transfer in PbRC. Geometrically this implies that the center-to-center distance of $\check{\mathrm{P700}}$ to $\hat{\mathrm{Q_{K}}}$ is $25(\pm2)$ $\mathring\mathrm{A}$ $(25.5(\pm0.3)\$ ÊA by pulsed EPR; Bittl et al., 1997); the equivalent distance in PbRC is $28.2(\pm0.3)\$ ÊA. In a view from the p-side onto the membrane plane (Figure $\mathsf{4c}),$ ), the line $\mathrm{\DeltaQ_{A}}$ and $\mathrm{Q_{B}}$ is rotated in a counterclockwise direction as compared to $\scriptstyle\mathrm{Q}_{\mathrm{K}}$ in PSI, mirroring a similar but more pronounced rotation of the pheophytins of PbRC as compared to $\mathrm{eC}_{3}$ and $\mathrm{e}\hat{\mathrm{C}}_{3}^{'}$ in PSI (see above).
If P700 is taken as a unit, the centers of the cofactors of the electron transfer system of PSI are seen to roughly describe a plane perpendicular to the membrane plane (Figure 3c). The phylloquinone $\scriptstyle\mathrm{Q}_{\mathrm{K}}$ lies immediately adjacent to this plane. This contrasts with PbRC, where the center of the primary electron donor, the accessory BChls and quinones are approximately coplanar while the bacteriopheophytins lie adjacent to the plane.
## Iron cofactors of the electron transfer systems
The position of the ®rst iron-sulfur cluster in PSI, $\mathrm{F}_{\mathrm{X}},$ roughly corresponds to the non-heme iron (Fe) in purple bacteria. In analogy to $\mathrm{F}_{\mathrm{X}},$ located on the pseudo-2-fold axis ${\mathrm{C}}_{2}({\mathrm{AB}}).$ , Fe is positioned on the pseudo-2-fold axis ${\mathrm{C}}_{2}(\mathrm{LM})$ relating the subunits L and M. However, apart from $\mathrm{F_{x}}$ being a more complex structure, Fe is also located $4\mathring\mathrm{A}$ nearer the primary electron donor than the center of $\mathrm{F_{x}}$ . While an active participation of Fe in electron transfer from $\mathrm{\DeltaQ_{A}}$ to $\scriptstyle\mathbf{Q}_{\mathrm{B}}$ in PbRC has not been demonstrated, $\mathrm{F}_{\mathrm{X}}$ is clearly located in a position both compatible with and indicative of participation in electron transfer to $\mathrm{F_{B}}$ and $\mathrm{F_{A}}$ (Krauû et al., 1993). This has been con®rmed experimentally (L Èuneberg et al., 1994; van der Est et al., 1994; MoeÈnne-Loccoz et al., 1994).
The terminal $\mathrm{Fe}_{4}S_{4}$ -clusters $\mathrm{F_{A}}$ and $\mathrm{F_{B}}$ of PSI (denoted $\mathrm{F}_{1}$ and $\mathrm{F}_{2}$ in Figure 3) do not belong to the membrane-integral part of the electron transfer system but are instead co-ordinated by the n-side subunit PsaC. These centers represent the de®nitive difference from the type-II RCs, resulting in the synonymous use of iron-sulfur-type for type-I RCs.
## Comparison of the core $\pmb{\alpha}$ -helices of PSI and PbRC
In both the RC-cores of PSI and the ${\mathrm{Pb}}{\mathrm{RC}},$ ten transmembrane $\textsf{\textsf{Q}}$ -helices from two pseudosymmetrical, partly homologous subunits (Figures 2 and 5) surround an inner space that accommodates the cofactors of the electron transfer system (Figure 4). The $\textsf{\textsf{q}}$ -helices A, B, C, E and D of each subunit L and M (PbRC) correspond directly to $\underline{{\mathbf{i}}},\underline{{\mathbf{j}}},\underline{{\mathbf{k}}},$ o and m of PsaA and PsaB of PSI (Figures 4 and 5). In both PSI and PbRC these ten $\textsf{\textsf{Q}}$ -helices (the \`\`palisade'') function to both support the cofactors in their appropriate orientations and to spatially separate them from the immediate surroundings. Whereas the arrangement is more \`\`loosely'' packed in PbRC (see the black lines connecting red $\mathfrak{a}$ -helices in Figure 4), the palisade encircles the cofactors more tightly in PSI (blue $\textsf{\textsf{Q}}$ -helices in Figure 4) with the $\textsf{\textsf{Q}}$ -helices $\underline{{\mathbf{k}}},\mathbf{j}$ and $\underline{{\dot{1}}}$ wrapping around the electron transfer system such that $\underline{{\dot{1}}}$ is near $\textsf{\textsf{Q}}$ -helix $\underline{{\mathbf{m}}}^{\prime}$ of the opposite subunit. This is most pronounced near the $\mathrm{\sf~p~}$ -side (Figure 4a) with the arrangement, especially for those $\textsf{\textsf{Q}}$ -helices most distant from the pseudo-2-fold axes, becoming quite similar near the $\mathfrak{n}$ -side surface (Figure 4c).
Nevertheless, some of the $\mathfrak{a}$ -helices involved differ signi®cantly in their relative orientation and position. (i) Starting at the $\mathrm{\tt~p\/}$ -side near the primary donors (Figure 4a), the $\textsf{\textsf{Q}}$ -helices $l$ and $l^{\prime}$ are seen to spatially correspond quite well to Lcd and Mcd of PbRC. Their lengths are similar, as are their orientations. In accordance with a tighter packing of the RC in PSI, l and $l^{\prime}$ are mutually somewhat closer than their counterparts Lcd and Mcd of PbRC. (ii) The $\mathfrak{a}$ -helices m and $\mathbf{m}^{\prime}$ of PSI similarly function to co-ordinate the primary electron donor, as do $\textsf{\textsf{q}}$ -helices LD and MD in PbRC. The position of m (and $\mathbf{m}^{\prime}$ ) near $\mathrm{eC_{1}}$ $(\mathrm{eC_{1}}^{\prime})$ closely mirrors that of LD (MD) near $\mathrm{\DeltaD_{L}}$ $(\mathrm{D}_{\mathrm{M}};\mathrm{~\boldmath~\Omega~})$ Figures $\mathsf{4}\mathsf{\check{b}}$ and 6), indicating that the mode and geometry of $\mathrm{(B)Chl}$ co-ordination is likely to be analogous in both Rcs. (iii) Despite this similarity of function, LD and MD of PbRC extend signi®cantly further towards the nside surface than m and $\dot{\mathbf{m}}^{\prime}$ of PSI. Due to their strong inclination, the length of LD and MD leads to a \`\`crossing-over'' of each $\textsf{\textsf{Q}}$ -helix into the region occupied by the opposite subunit, structurally locking the subunits onto each other (Figures 4c,6). Because of the presence of $\mathrm{F}_{\mathrm{X}},$ m and $\bar{\mathbf{m}}^{\prime}$ end well short of the n-side surface. Instead of crossing-over they continue after a short bend into the $\textsf{\textsf{Q}}$ -helices n and $\ensuremath{\mathbf{n}}^{\prime}$ (Figure 4c), which are positioned at an equivalent membrane depth as $\mathrm{F}_{\mathrm{x}}{\mathrm{ $ and are close to the n-side ends of $\mathrm{~j~}$ and $\underline{{\dot{1}}}$ thereby performing a similar stabilizing function as LD and LM. (iv) Sequentially, $\textsf{\textsf{Q}}$ -helices $\underline{{\mathfrak{n}}}$ of PSI map to the $\mathfrak{Q}.$ - helices de (and de') of PbRC (Figures $\bar{4}\mathrm{c},5$ ), being intermediate between m and o on the one hand and $\mathrm{~D~}$ and E on the other. Structurally, the $\mathfrak{a}$ -helix lengths and positions of PbRC are however not comparable, with $\underline{{\mathfrak{n}}}$ being appreciably longer and essentially parallel with the membrane plane,
