## Sequence comparison between RC proteins of PbRC and PSI
A comparison of the $\textsf{\textsf{Q}}$ -helix connectivity in PbRC and the RC-domain of PSI (PSI-RC) indicates that the arrangement of $\textsf{\textsf{Q}}$ -helices is not analogous only in the structural sense of surrounding and enclosing the electron transfer chain. Instead, the relative connectivity of the $\textsf{\textsf{q}}$ -helices involved is also analogous (Figure 5). This represents the most fundamental support of the hypothesis that both systems share a common evolutionary origin and were not arrived at by convergent evolution. Comparisons of primary structures had previously proposed a number of homologous regions for both structures (Robert & MoeÈnne-Loccoz, 1990;
Margulies, 1991; Otsuka et al., 1992; Kuhn et al., 1994), though the reliability of these predictions could not be veri®ed. Most of these directly contradict the proposed sequence assignment of PsaA and PsaB to the X-ray structure model of PSI (Krauû et al., 1996; Schubert et al., 1997) and will not be discussed further, while only one appears to re¯ect a possible earlier correspondence.
The prediction by Margulies (1991); and see Scheme 1) maps the hydrophobic span X of PsaB of PSI (m in the present PSI model) to the $\mathfrak{a}$ -helix D of subunits L and M of PbRC, which is responsible for co-ordinating the BChl of the primary electron donor. This is broadly in agreement with the structural model of PSI, as the latter similarly implicates $\textsf{\textsf{Q}}$ -helix m in co-ordinating the Chla of P700.
A major drawback of the homology is that it largely rests on a four amino acid residue motif, YNPF, at the N-terminal end of $\textsf{\textsf{Q}}$ -helix LD and MD of PbRC and GbRC that appears to have been conserved in PsaB of PSI and partialy in D1 and D2 of PSII, and marginally in PsaA of PSI.
