Aligning both the YNPF-motif and the histidine residue co-ordinating the primary electron donor, (B)Chl would necessitate a 15 residue deletion/ insertion in moving from PsaB (and other type-I core proteins) to L and M (or other type-II proteins) or vice versa. Interestingly, the positions within the membrane, the relative inclinations (taking the membrane normal as reference) and lengths of $\mathfrak{a}$ -helices $\underline{{\mathbf{m}}}$ of PSI and $\mathrm{~D~}$ of PbRC differ quite considerably (Figure 6). Thus the 15 residue insertion could explain the fact that the proposed histidine residues in $\textsf{\textsf{Q}}$ -helix $\underline{{\mathbf{m}}}$ of PsaA and PsaB are positioned near the center of this $\mathfrak{X}$ -helix in PSI $(\sim1\hat{2}\pm3$ amino acid residues from the N-terminal end) rather than at the $\mathrm{\sf~p~}$ -side end as in $\textsf{\textsf{Q}}$ -helix D (PbRC). This would not per se, however, account for the C-terminal (n-side) extension of $\mathfrak{a}$ -helix D (PbRC) as compared to m (PSI). An alternative explanation would be that a frame-shift after the YNPF motif fortuitously introduced an alternative histidine position. As a result the histidine residue previously responsible for primary donor co-ordination would be moved either from the center of the co-ordinating $\textsf{\textsf{Q}}$ -helix (as in PSI) to the end (as in PbRC) or again vice versa. As the position of the primary electron donor relative to the remaining cofactors of the electron transfer system would clearly not tolerate signi®cant changes, only an accompanying shift of the $\textsf{\textsf{Q}}$ -helix as a whole would guarantee a workable RC. Clearly, the chance of this type of cataclysmic frame-shift resulting in a new viable RC is extremely low. However, if it did happen, it could result in the coevolution of the characteristic differences between type-I and type-II RCs including (i) the replacement and positional shifting of the iron-sulfur cluster $\mathrm{F}_{\mathrm{X}}$ (type-I) or its counterpart Fe (type-II), (ii) the substitution in the third pophyrin pair of a (bacterio)chlorophyll (type-I) by a (bacterio)pheophytin (type-II), (iii) the positional shift of the quinone acceptor pairs and (iv) the observed differences in the position of the $\mathfrak{a}$ -helix responsible for co-ordinating the primary electron donor. The cause for such a frame-shift could either be a coincidental mutation or the positive pressure resulting from a previous change, including for argument's sake the loss of the structurally dominating iron-sulfur cluster.
Aligning the histidine residues co-ordinating the primary electron donor (B)Chls in Scheme 1 does not, however, reveal any additional conserved residues C-terminal to the YNPF motif between type-I and type-II core proteins. This would have provided the decisive proof of the insertion/deletion hypothesis. The frame-shift hypothesis, however, remains no less speculative and will be not be discussed further at this time. However some additional points do arise from a sequence comparison in the region immediately N-terminal to $\textsf{\textsf{Q}}$ -helices m and D in PSI and PbRC respectively. (i) The proposed sequence assignment to the structural model of PSI equates the sequence intermediate to the hydrophobic spans IX and X $\mathrm{\Delta}\cdot\mathrm{\Delta}\propto$ -helix l and the adjoining residues) of PSI to the $\textsf{\textsf{Q}}$ -helix cd in PbRC. In contrast to PbRC, this region does not contain a histidine residue (Scheme 1), reminiscent of a similar situation noted previously in PSII (Trebst, 1986; Michel et al., 1986; Michel & Deisenhofer, 1988). Thus the accessory Chla appears not to be co-ordinated by the $\mathfrak{a}$ -helix l in both oxygenic RCs, at least not by a histidine residue. This lack of a co-ordinating histidine residue in l is in agreement with the positions and orientations of $\check{\mathrm{eC}}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{eC}_{2}^{\prime}.$ , which are quite different from that of the $\mathtt{B_{A}}$ and $\mathsf{B}_{\mathrm{B}}$ in PbRC in having a much lower inclination relative to the membrane normal and in being located signi®cantly deeper within the membrane (see above). The earlier observation of sequence homology between $\textsf{\textsf{q}}$ -helix CD of PbRC and span VIII of PSI (Kuhn et al., 1994) is thus, in retrospect, seen to have been coincidental.
Interestingly, a histidine residue is present in the region intermediate to the hydrophobic spans IX and X in Heliobacillus mobilis and Chlorobium limicola, respectively, which would correspond to the $\mathfrak{a}$ -helix cd in PbRC. Though there is very little sequence homology in the adjoining stretches to support the correspondence of these sequences in PshA, PscA and $\bar{\mathrm{~L~}}$ and M, the histidine residue could indicate that the accessory BChl in green sulfur bacteria and heliobacteria could be positioned in a manner reminiscent of PbRC. This in turn could indicate that histidine co-ordination by the $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{\tt~p\}}$ -side $\textsf{\textsf{Q}}$ -helix was lost in PSI and PSII following the evolution of oxygenic photosynthesis.
## Implications for other photosynthetic systems
Despite low overall sequence homology $(\sim15\%)$ between the PSI core proteins, PsaA/PsaB (PSI), PshA (PSH, Liebl et al., 1993) and PscA (PSC, B Èuttner et al., 1992), it is suf®ciently high in speci®c regions (including the $\mathrm{F}_{\mathrm{X}}$ binding sequences) to indicate a common evolutionary origin for type-I RCs (B Èuttner et al., 1992). The core proteins of type-II RCs are similarly partly homologous (Rochaix et al., 1984; Michel et al., 1986; Beanland, 1990) and have allowed detailed models of PSII to be proposed based on the structure of PbRC (Michel & Deisenhofer, 1988; Trebst, 1986; Svensson et al., 1991, 1996; Ruf¯e et al., 1992; Xiong et al., 1996). By contrast, ®rm structural or genetic evidence proving a shared ancestry for the two alternative RC types has remained elusive. Though homologies have been noted (Fish et al., 1985; Robert & MoÈenne-Loccoz, 1990; Margulies, 1991; Otsuka et al., 1992; Kuhn et al., 1994), their validity has been dif®cult to substantiate.
The far-reaching analogy observed in both the electron transfer chains and the core protein structure of PSI and PbRC (see above) now ®lls this void by clearly demonstrating a common structural blue-print and thus a common evolutionary origin for all RC-cores. This in turn implies that all RCcores, including PSII, PSH and PSC, are presumably constituted by a (pseudo)symmetric scaffolding of two sets of ®ve transmembrane $\textsf{\textsf{Q}}$ -helices that in turn surround and support two (pseudo)- symmetric branches of cofactors of the ETS grouped around a central $\mathrm{(pseudo)}C_{2}$ axis. The set of cofactors in each case includes: (i) a pair of (bacterio)chlorophyll molecules constituting the primary electron donor (see limitations in regard of PSII discussed above); (ii) a second pair of (bacterio)chlorophyll molecules, the so-called accessory or monomeric chlorophylls; (iii) a third pair of (bacterio)pheophytin or chlorophyll molecules, at least one functioning in electron transfer; (iv) two quinone molecules, either one or both of which are active in electron transfer (this may not be true of green sulfur RCs); and (v) an iron species: nonheme $\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}$ (sometimes $\mathbf{Mg}^{2+}.$ , Arnoux et al., 1995) in quinone type RCs and $\mathrm{Fe}_{4}S_{4}$ -clusters active in electron transfer in iron-sulfur type RCs.
## Photosystem II
In PSII, the central RC-core constituted by the subunits D1 and D2 is complemented by an outer core antenna carried by the Chla co-ordinating proteins CP43 and CP47 (for a review, see Bricker, 1990). This core antenna is structurally independent of the light-harvesting complex LHCII found in higher plant PSII (KÈuhlbrandt et al., 1994). In contrast to all known type-I RCcomplexes, which combine an RC and an antenna-domain in a single protein subunit, the core antenna-carrying subunits of PSII are distinct from the core-proteins D1 and D2 (Figure 5). The sequence homology between D1/D2 (PSII) and $\mathrm{L}/\mathrm{\bar{M}}$ (PbRC, see above) has allowed a model of the PSII to be postulated based on the RC-core structure of PbRC (Ruf¯e et al., 1992; Svensson et al., 1996; Xiong et al., 1996).
The RC-core of PSII deviates from that of PbRC, however, by possessing two additional \`\`extra'' or \`\`peripheral'' Chla in addition to the four Chla and 2 pheophytins that mirror the electron transfer system of PbRC (Kobayashi et al., 1990; Gounaris et al.,
1990). They are part of the RC-core preparations and would appear to be co-ordinated by the RCdomain proteins D1 and D2. Apart from D1, D2, CP43 and CP47, none of the subunits of PSII has been shown to bind Chla of the core antenna or electron transfer systems.
Signi®cant homology has been reported for the ®rst and fourth hydrophobic sequences of the coreantenna binding protein CP47 (PSII) and PshA (PSH; Vermaas, 1994). As both CP47 and CP43 contain six hydrophobic spans (presumably transmembrane $\mathfrak{a}$ -helices; Bricker, 1990), the observed homology indicates that the six N-terminal hydrophobic spans of PshA (of a total of 11) form a coreantenna-binding domain equivalent to CP47. The ®ve remaining, C-terminal hydrophobic spans of PshA would then presumably form an RC-domain equivalent to the partly homologous D1 and D2 proteins of PSII, though meaningful sequence homology to prove this latter point was not available at the time. A similar hypothesis for an N-terminal core-antenna-binding domain and a C-terminal RC-domain in the RC-proteins PsaA and PsaB of PSI (partly homologous to PshA of PSH) was put forward on the basis of structural arguments based on the ®rst $6\textup{\AA}$ structural model of PSI (Fromme et al., 1994). Though the corresponding homology between CP47 and PsaA/PsaB was reported to be less signi®cant (Vermaas, 1994), this is not necessarily the case. In fact CP43, CP47, PshA, PsaA and PsaB all share signi®cant sequence homology locally, $62\%$ between spans I and V of PsaA/PsaB (PSI) and CP43/CP47 (PSII; Scheme 2, Fromme et al., 1996), despite low overall homology.
Compiling the available evidence to include both the structural and sequential homologies indicates that both the RC-domains and the core antenna domain structures of all RC-complexes (where present) each have a common origin, while it appears likely that the ancestral progenitor of all photosynthetic systems already combined the two in a proto-photosystem. Thus, apart from serving as a model for PSH and PSC, the existing structural model of PSI is of relevance in extending the present model of the PSII RC (Trebst, 1986; Michel & Deisenhofer, 1988; Svensson et al., 1992; Ruf¯e et al., 1992; Xiong et al., 1996) to include CP43 and CP47 (as ®rst proposed by Fromme et al., 1996). The following predictions for the PSII structure result from the above (Figure 7): (i) PSII possesses a domain structure similar to that of PSI with a central RC-domain surrounded by a core antenna domain. (ii) The $\mathfrak{a}$ -helix arrangement of the RCdomain is more loosely packed, resembling PbRC rather than PSI. (iii) The transmembrane $\textsf{\textsf{Q}}$ -helices arrangement of CP43 and CP47 consists of a similar trimer-of-dimers structure as that observed for the six N-terminal $\textsf{\textsf{Q}}$ -helices of PsaA and PsaB. (iv) CP43 and CP47 are positioned on opposite sides of the PSII-RC i.e. excitation energy is presumably transferred from each directly to PSII-RC and not one via the other. (v) The number of Chla associated with the six N-terminal $\textsf{\textsf{Q}}$ -helices of each
