A pair of roughly symmetrically positioned Chla, $\mathsf{\hat{a}C_{1}}$ and $\mathsf{a C}_{5},$ of the core antenna system in PSI are located adjacent to $\mathfrak{Q}$ -helix j and $\ensuremath{\mathbf{j}}^{\prime}$ . They are oriented largely parallel with the membrane normal and positioned at a membrane depth intermediate between $\underline{{\mathrm{eC}_{2}}}$ and $\mathrm{eC}_{3}$ . Their respective center-to-center distances to $\overline{{{\mathrm{eC_{1}}}}}/{{\mathrm{eC_{1}}}^{\prime}}$ are $30/31\mathrm{~\AA~}$ and to $\mathrm{eC}_{3}/\mathrm{eC}_{3}^{\prime}$ are $16/18\mathrm{\mathring{A}},$ respectively.
Thus if the extra Chla molecules of PSII are indeed co-ordinated by the conserved histidine residues D1/D2-118, then these Chla are not equivalent to the connecting Chla of PSI. In this case, they may correspond to other core-antenna Chla of PSI (e.g. $\mathsf{a C}_{1}$ and $\mathsf{a C}_{5})$ . Alternatively, if the accessory Chla of PSII are co-ordinated by these conserved histidine residues, their positions would likely be far removed from the other cofactors of the electron transfer system, as proposed by Mulkidjanian et al. (1996).
