(ii) The mode of accessory Chl co-ordination in PSH (Heliobacillus mobilis) and PSC (Chlorobium vibrioforme) appears similar to that of PbRC and partially to GbRC (Chloro¯exus aurantiacus). In both PshA and PscA, a histidine residue is conserved in the intermediate sequence region between the hydrophobic spans IX and X: H501 in PshA and H586 in PscA, which according to the proposed sequence assignment of PSI (Krauû et al., 1996; Schubert et al., 1997) corresponds to the amphiphilic $\mathfrak{a}$ -helix cd of PbRC (Scheme 1, left-hand rectangle). The corresponding regions of PSI (see above) and PSII (Michel et al., 1986; Trebst, 1986) lack this histidine residue. The residues adjacent to the histidine residues are partly conserved as compared to PbRC and GbRC, slightly more so in the case of PscA than PshA:
$$
\begin{array}{r}{\mathrm{~M\left(~Ca\right)~-\mathrm{LLDWT}}}\\ {\mathrm{~M\left(~RV\right)~-\mathrm{HZDWL}}}\\ {\mathrm{~L-HLDWV}}\\ {\mathrm{~Psc\&A-HLQHH}}\\ {\mathrm{~PshA-HAVSN}}\end{array}
$$
$\mathbf{M}(C a)$ and $\mathbf{M}(R v)$ are the M subunits of C. aurantiacus (GbRC) and Rhodospeudomonas viridis (PbRC), L the corresponding subunit in GbRC and PbRC.
Of particular interest in this context is the fact that in the green ®lamentous bacterium C. aurantiacus the accessory BChl co-ordinating histidine residue is conserved in only one of the core subunits (Shiozawa et al., 1989). The resulting accessory bacteriopheophytin is reminiscent of the functionally inactive $\mathtt{B_{B}}$ in PbRC.
In both PshA and $\mathrm{PscA},$ the hydrophobic span VIII, which corresponds to $\textsf{\textsf{q}}$ -helix B in PbRC and PSII, contains at least one histidine residue conserved also in PsaA and PsaB of PSI. As indicated above, these may correspond to the conserved histidine residues D1/D2-H118 (PSII). In PSI, however, they co-ordinate neither the accessory nor the connecting Chla of PSI (see above), indicating that the extra Chla of PSII (Vacha et al., 1995) may have their counterpart amongst the antenna $(\mathrm{B)Chl}$ of PSI, PSC and PSH.
The region proposed to co-ordinate the phylloquinones of PSI includes the $\mathbf{n}$ -side end of $\textsf{\textsf{Q}}$ -helix m (span X), the adjoining loop and the proximal end of $\textsf{\textsf{q}}$ -helix $\underline{{\mathbf{n}}}$ as well as the loop immediately n-side of $\textsf{\textsf{q}}$ -helix o (Schubert et al., 1997). It shares signi®cant homology with equivalent regions of both PshA and PscA, which may be summarized as $\operatorname{RGxxxx}$ (yellow underlaid residues near the right-hand side of Scheme 1). This indicates that the binding mode of the quinone electron acceptor may be similar in all type-I RCs. (This would contradict some reports, which indicate that PSC does not possess an active quinone electron acceptor; Hauska et al., 1995; Sakurai et al., 1996.)
The antenna size of both PSH and PSC are likely to be very similar to that of PSII (i.e. about 40 to 50 Chl), as in each system the same number of transmembrane $\mathsf{\Omega}\propto$ -helices is available to co-ordinate the Chl. This is comparable to the 23 to 25 Chla directly associated with the six N-terminal $\textsf{\textsf{q}}$ -helices of PsaA and PsaB (Krauû et al., 1996; Schubert et al., 1997). In PSI, the additional ${\sim}30$ to 40 Chla of PSI are accommodated by the smaller, peripheral, membrane-integral subunits, resulting in an appreciably larger core antenna in PSI in comparison to the other photosystems.
## Conclusion
The present model of cyanobacterial PSI has for the ®rst time permitted a systematic comparison of type-I and type-II RC complexes. In particular it reveals an RC-core unit common to all photosynthetic systems. Thus, PSI and PbRC share analogous structural blue-prints both as regards the cofactor composition and their arrangement. As the differences between the two structures are quite large, despite many analogies being immediately obvious, a comparison of structures determined at higher resolution would not necessarily have produced a signi®cantly different result. The only exception to this may be the comparison of the primary electron donor (B)Chl pair, which appears now to be far more similar in the two structures than had previously appeared to be the case. At present, the resolution of the PSI electron density maps is insuf®cient to quantify any differences that may exist in this respect.
Sequence comparisons of RC proteins and core antenna proteins of all known photosynthetic, bacterial phyla and from algae and higher plants have revealed many functionally important correspondences between different RCs and the antennae. More may yet be found, however, either when PSI is modelled at higher resolution and when model structures of other representatives of the family of photosynthetic reaction center complexes become available. This would provide a wider sampling base from which to judge the signi®cance of individual observations.
